lope -1/3 covers (1,2) and (4,1). That's okay.\n\nThus we have two options for L2: slope -0.5 covering (3,1), or slope -1/3 covering (4,1). We need to cover both (3,1) and (4,1) and (3,2). But we have only one line left (L2). So we cannot cover both (3,1) and (4,1) with L2. So we need to see if any of the other lines can cover (3,2) or (3,1) or (4,1). Let's check.\n\n- L1 slope 2 cannot cover (3,2) or (3,1) or (4,1).\n- L3 slope -2 cannot cover (3,2) or (3,1) or (4,1).\n- L4 slope -2 cannot cover (3,2) or (3,1) or (4,1).\n\nThus only L2 can cover any of those points. So we cannot cover all three points (3,1),(3,2),(4,1) with a single line. So we need to adjust our earlier assignments to free up another line to cover one of those points.\n\nThus we need to assign at least two lines to cover column 3 and column 4 points. Since we have 4 lines total, we need to allocate at least two lines to cover column 3 points (3,1) and (3,2), and possibly column 4 point (4,1). So we need to assign slopes to at least two lines that intersect column 3 at integer points.\n\nThus we need to assign slopes to lines such that they intersect column 3 at integer points. For a line i, column 3 point is (3, b_i + 2 m_i). For this to be integer, 2 m_i must be integer (i.e., m_i rational with denominator dividing 2). So m_i can be integer or half-integer.\n\nThus we need at least two lines with m_i integer or half-integer such that b_i + 2 m_i ∈ {1,2}.\n\nLet's try to assign slopes accordingly.\n\nWe have lines L1 (b1=1), L2 (b2=2), L3 (b3=3), L4 (b4=4). We need to assign slopes m_i such that:\n\n- For each i, m_i ≠ 0, -1.\n- For each i, we may have column 2 integer point if m_i integer.\n- For each i, we may have column 3 integer point if 2 m_i integer.\n- For each i, we may have column 4 integer point if 3 m_i integer.\n\nWe need to cover all points.\n\nLet's try to assign slopes as follows:\n\n- L1: slope m1 = 1 (integer). Then column 2 point (2,2) covered. Column 3 point (3, 1 + 2*1) = (3,3) not allowed. So L1 covers (1,1) and (2,2).\n\n- L2: slope m2 = -1/2 (half-integer). Then column 2 point (2, 2 - 0.5) = (2,1.5) not integer, so no column 2 coverage. Column 3 point (3, 2 + 2*(-0.5)) = (3,1) integer, allowed. Column 4 point (4, 2 + 3*(-0.5)) = (4,0.5) not integer. So L2 covers (1,2) and (3,1).\n\n- L3: slope m3 = -2 (integer). Then column 2 point (2, 3 - 2) = (2,1) integer. Column 3 point (3, 3 + 2*(-2)) = (3,-1) not allowed. So L3 covers (1,3) and (2,1).\n\n- L4: slope m4 = -2/3 (rational with denominator 3). Then column 2 point (2, 4 - 2/3) = (2, 10/3) not integer. Column 3 point (3, 4 + 2*(-2/3)) = (3, 4 - 4/3) = (3, 8/3) not integer. Column 4 point (4, 4 + 3*(-2/3)) = (4, 4 - 2) = (4,2) not allowed (b=2 >1). So L4 only covers (1,4). Not enough.\n\nWe still need to cover (2,2) (covered by L1), (2,3) not covered, (3,2) not covered, (4,1) not covered. So we need to adjust.\n\nLet's try to assign L4 slope m4 = -1/3 (to cover (4,1)). Then L4 covers (1,4) and (4,1). Column 2 point (2, 4 - 1/3) = (2, 11/3) not integer. Column 3 point (3, 4 - 2/3) = (3, 10/3) not integer. So L4 covers only (1,4) and (4,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (2,3) and (3,2). We have L1 covering (2,2), L3 covering (2,1), L2 covering (3,1). So we need to cover (2,3) and (3,2). We have no lines left. So we need to adjust.\n\nPerhaps we can assign L1 slope m1 = 2 to cover (2,3). Then L1 covers (1,1) and (2,3). Column 3 point (3, 1 + 2*2) = (3,5) not allowed. So L1 covers (1,1),(2,3). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (2,2) and (2,1) and (3,2) and (3,1) and (4,1). L3 slope -2 covers (2,1). L2 slope -1/2 covers (3,1). L4 slope -1/3 covers (4,1). So we have (2,2) and (3,2) left. We could adjust L3 to cover (2,2) instead of (2,1). For L3 (b3=3), to cover (2,2) we need m3 = -1, prohibited. So L3 cannot cover (2,2). L2 could cover (2,2) if m2 = 0, prohibited. L1 slope 2 cannot cover (2,2). L4 slope -1/3 cannot cover (2,2). So we cannot cover (2,2). So this fails.\n\nThus we need a different assignment.\n\nLet's try to assign L1 slope m1 = 1 (covers (2,2)). L2 slope m2 = -1/2 (covers (3,1)). L3 slope m3 = -2 (covers (2,1)). L4 slope m4 = -1/3 (covers (4,1)). Then we still need to cover (2,3) and (3,2). We have no lines left. So we need to adjust to cover those.\n\nMaybe we can assign L2 slope m2 = 1 (covers (2,3) and (3,4) not allowed). Actually L2 slope 1 gives column 2 point (2,3) covered, column 3 point (3,4) not allowed. So L2 covers (1,2) and (2,3). Then we need to cover (3,1) and (3,2). L1 slope 1 covers (2,2) but not (3,2). L3 slope -2 covers (2,1) but not (3,2). L4 slope -1/3 covers (4,1). So we still need to cover (3,1) and (3,2). We could try to adjust L1 slope to cover (3,2) as well. For L1 (b1=1), to cover (3,2) we need 1 + 2 m1 = 2 => 2 m1 = 1 => m1 = 0.5, allowed. Then column 2 point (2, 1 + 0.5) = (2,1.5) not integer. So L1 would cover (1,1) and (3,2). Then we need to cover (2,2) maybe with L3? L3 slope -2 covers (2,1). L3 cannot cover (2,2). L2 slope 1 covers (2,3). L4 slope -1/3 covers (4,1). So we still need (2,2) and (3,1). L3 could be changed to slope -1? Not allowed. L3 could be slope -0.5? Let's see: L3 (b3=3) slope -0.5 gives column 2 point (2, 3 - 0.5) = (2,2.5) not integer. Column 3 point (3, 3 + 2*(-0.5)) = (3,2) integer, but (3,2) already covered by L1. So L3 not helpful.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nWe could try to assign L1 slope 0.5 (covers (3,2)), L2 slope 1 (covers (2,3)), L3 slope -2 (covers (2,1)), L4 slope -1/3 (covers (4,1)). Then we need to cover (2,2) and (3,1). L1 slope 0.5 does not cover (2,2). L2 slope 1 does not cover (3,1). L3 slope -2 does not cover (3,1). L4 slope -1/3 does not cover (3,1). So we need to adjust.\n\nMaybe we can assign L2 slope -0.5 (covers (3,1)), L1 slope 1 (covers (2,2)), L3 slope -2 (covers (2,1)), L4 slope -1/3 (covers (4,1)). Then we need to cover (2,3) and (3,2). L1 slope 1 does not cover (3,2). L2 slope -0.5 does not cover (2,3). L3 slope -2 does not cover (3,2). L4 slope -1/3 does not cover (3,2). So fails.\n\nThus we need to find a combination where we can cover (2,3) and (3,2) with the same line perhaps. Let's see if any line can cover both (2,3) and (3,2). The line through (2,3) and (3,2) has slope (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, prohibited. So cannot.\n\nCan any line cover both (2,2) and (3,2)? That would be horizontal line y=2, prohibited. So cannot.\n\nCan any line cover both (2,1) and (3,2)? Yes, slope 1, allowed. That's line y = x - 1. It passes through (2,1) and (3,2). So we can use a line to cover both (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\nCan any line cover both (2,3) and (3,1)? Slope (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, allowed. That's line y = -2x + 7. It passes through (2,3) and (3,1). Good.\n\nThus we can cover (2,1)-(3,2) with one line, and (2,3)-(3,1) with another line. Then we need to cover (2,2) and (4,1) and column 1 points. Let's try to assign lines accordingly.\n\nWe have 4 lines total. Let's assign:\n\n- L1: covers (2,1)-(3,2). This line passes through (2,1) and (3,2). Its equation: y = x - 1 (slope 1). It also passes through (1,0) not needed. So L1 does not intersect column 1 at any integer point. So L1 does not cover any column 1 point. That's okay, but we need to cover column 1 points with other lines.\n\n- L2: covers (2,3)-(3,1). Equation: y = -2x + 7. At a=1, y = 5, not allowed (1+5=6 >5). So L2 does not intersect column 1 at integer point. So L2 also does not cover column 1.\n\nThus we have used 2 lines that do not cover column 1 points. We have 2 lines left to cover column 1 points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Since each line can intersect column 1 at exactly one point, we can cover at most 2 of those points with the remaining 2 lines. So we cannot cover all 4 column 1 points. Therefore we need to assign at least one of the lines covering column 1 points to also cover some of the other points. So we need to find a line that passes through a column 1 point and also covers some of the other points (like (2,1)-(3,2) or (2,3)-(3,1) or (4,1)). Let's see if we can find a line that passes through (1,b) and also passes through (2,1) and (3,2). That would be a line through (1,b) and (2,1). The slope would be (1 - b)/(2 - 1) = 1 - b. For b=1, slope = 0, prohibited. For b=2, slope = -1, prohibited. For b=3, slope = -2, allowed. So line through (1,3) and (2,1) has slope -2. Does it also pass through (3,2)? Let's check: line equation: y - 3 = -2 (x - 1) => y = -2x + 5. At x=3, y = -6 + 5 = -1, not 2. So it does not pass through (3,2). So line (1,3)-(2,1) covers (1,3) and (2,1). It does not cover (3,2). So we could use this line to cover (2,1) and a column 1 point (1,3). Then we need to cover (3,2) separately.\n\nAlternatively, line through (1,b) and (3,2). Slope = (2 - b)/(3 - 1) = (2 - b)/2. For b=1, slope = (2-1)/2 = 0.5, allowed. So line through (1,1) and (3,2) slope 0.5. Does it also pass through (2,1)? Let's check: equation: y - 1 = 0.5 (x - 1) => y = 0.5 x + 0.5. At x=2, y = 1.5, not integer. So line (1,1)-(3,2) covers (1,1) and (3,2). It does not cover (2,1). So we could use this line to cover (3,2) and column 1 point (1,1). Then we need to cover (2,1) separately.\n\nThus we can try to assign lines as follows:\n\n- L1: line through (1,1) and (3,2) slope 0.5. Covers (1,1) and (3,2). (Covers column 1 point (1,1) and column 3 point (3,2).)\n\n- L2: line through (1,3) and (2,1) slope -2. Covers (1,3) and (2,1). (Covers column 1 point (1,3) and column 2 point (2,1).)\n\n- L3: line through (2,3) and (3,1) slope -2. Covers (2,3) and (3,1). (Covers column 2 point (2,3) and column 3 point (3,1).)\n\n- L4: line through (1,4) and (4,1) slope -1 (prohibited). So cannot use that. We need to cover (4,1) and (1,2) and (1,4) maybe with a line.\n\nWe still need to cover (1,2),(1,4),(2,2),(4,1). We have used L1, L2, L3. L1 covers (1,1),(3,2). L2 covers (1,3),(2,1). L3 covers (2,3),(3,1). So remaining points: (1,2),(1,4),(2,2),(4,1). We have one line left (L4) to cover these four points. That's impossible because a line can intersect each column at most once, so it can cover at most one point in column 1 (either (1,2) or (1,4)), at most one point in column 2 (maybe (2,2)), at most one point in column 4 (maybe (4,1)). So it can cover at most 3 of these points, leaving one uncovered. So we need to adjust earlier assignments to free up a line that can cover more of these points.\n\nAlternatively, we could try to assign L3 to cover (1,4) and (4,1) maybe? Let's see if there is a line through (1,4) and (4,1) with slope -1, prohibited. So cannot.\n\nMaybe we can assign L3 to cover (1,2) and (4,1) with a different slope. Let's see: line through (1,2) and (4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, allowed. Equation: y - 2 = -1/3 (x - 1) => y = -1/3 x + 2 + 1/3 = -1/3 x + 7/3. At x=2, y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3, not integer. At x=3, y = -1 + 7/3 = 4/3, not integer. So line covers only (1,2) and (4,1). Good. So we can use a line to cover (1,2) and (4,1). That line is L4: slope -1/3.\n\nNow we need to cover (1,4) and (2,2). We have L1 covering (1,1),(3,2). L2 covering (1,3),(2,1). L3 covering (2,3),(3,1). L4 covering (1,2),(4,1). So remaining points: (1,4) and (2,2). We have no lines left. So we need to adjust assignments to cover these.\n\nWe could try to adjust L1 to cover (1,4) instead of (1,1). Let's see if we can find a line through (1,4) and (3,2) maybe? Slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, prohibited. So cannot.\n\nLine through (1,4) and (2,2) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. Equation: y - 4 = -2 (x - 1) => y = -2x + 6. At x=3, y = 0, not allowed. So line covers (1,4) and (2,2). That's good. So we could assign L1 to be line through (1,4) and (2,2) slope -2. Then L1 covers (1,4) and (2,2). Then we need to cover (1,1) and (3,2) maybe with another line. Let's see.\n\nWe have L2 covering (1,3),(2,1). L3 covering (2,3),(3,1). L4 covering (1,2),(4,1). L1 covering (1,4),(2,2). Remaining points: (1,1),(3,2). We have no lines left. So we need to adjust L2 or L3 to cover (1,1) or (3,2). Let's see if we can modify L2 to cover (1,1) instead of (1,3). L2 currently is line through (1,3) and (2,1). Could we change L2 to line through (1,1) and (2,1)? That would be vertical line x=1, prohibited (non-sunny). So cannot. Could we change L2 to line through (1,1) and (3,2)? That's slope (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2, allowed. Equation: y - 1 = 0.5 (x - 1) => y = 0.5 x + 0.5. At x=2, y = 1.5, not integer. So line covers (1,1) and (3,2). That's good. So we can assign L2 to be line through (1,1) and (3,2) slope 0.5. Then L2 covers (1,1) and (3,2). Then we need to adjust L3 and L4 accordingly.\n\nNow we have:\n\n- L1: line through (1,4) and (2,2) slope -2 (covers (1,4),(2,2)).\n- L2: line through (1,1) and (3,2) slope 0.5 (covers (1,1),(3,2)).\n- L3: line through (2,3) and (3,1) slope -2 (covers (2,3),(3,1)).\n- L4: line through (1,2) and (4,1) slope -1/3 (covers (1,2),(4,1)).\n\nNow we need to check if all points are covered:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1) L2, (1,2) L4, (1,3) not covered, (1,4) L1. So (1,3) is missing.\n\nColumn 2: (2,1) not covered, (2,2) L1, (2,3) L3.\n\nColumn 3: (3,1) L3, (3,2) L2.\n\nColumn 4: (4,1) L4.\n\nMissing points: (1,3) and (2,1). We have no lines left. Could we adjust L3 to also cover (2,1) maybe? L3 currently covers (2,3) and (3,1). Could we change L3 to cover (2,1) and (3,1)? That would be vertical line x=1? No. (2,1) and (3,1) are horizontal line y=1, prohibited. So cannot. Could we change L3 to cover (2,1) and (2,3)? That's same column, not a line. So no.\n\nCould we adjust L1 to cover (2,1) instead of (2,2)? L1 currently covers (1,4) and (2,2). Could we change L1 to cover (1,4) and (2,1)? Slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, allowed. Equation: y - 4 = -3 (x - 1) => y = -3x + 7. At x=3, y = -9 + 7 = -2, not allowed. So L1 would cover (1,4) and (2,1). Then (2,2) would be uncovered. But maybe L3 could cover (2,2) instead of (2,3)? Let's see if L3 can cover (2,2) and (3,1). Slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, prohibited. So cannot.\n\nCould we adjust L4 to cover (1,3) instead of (1,2)? L4 currently covers (1,2) and (4,1). Could we change L4 to cover (1,3) and (4,1)? Slope = (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3, allowed. Equation: y - 3 = -2/3 (x - 1) => y = -2/3 x + 2/3 + 3 = -2/3 x + 11/3. At x=2, y = -4/3 + 11/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. So L4 would cover (1,3) and (4,1). Then (1,2) would be uncovered. But maybe L1 could cover (1,2) as well? L1 currently covers (1,4) and (2,1). Could we change L1 to cover (1,2) and (2,1)? Slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, prohibited. So cannot.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nWe can try other combinations. This is a complex combinatorial search. However, the fact that we are struggling to find a covering suggests that maybe it's impossible. But we need to be sure.\n\nGiven the difficulty, we suspect that k=4 is indeed impossible for n=5. But we need to verify for all n≥3. The solution's claim \"k≥4 impossible by column counting\" might be correct, but we need to verify the reasoning. The solution does not provide details, but the method \"column counting\" is a valid principle. However, we need to check if the claim is indeed correct. If the claim is false (i.e., there exists a configuration with k=4 for some n), then the answer is wrong. If the claim is correct, answer is correct.\n\nWe need to determine if there exists any n≥3 such that k=4 is possible. Let's try to search for small n manually.\n\nn=3: we have 3 lines, k=4 impossible because k cannot exceed n.\n\nn=4: we have 4 lines, k=4 means all lines sunny. We need to cover points (a,b) with a+b ≤ 5. Points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). We need 4 sunny lines covering all 10 points. We attempted earlier and found it impossible because the boundary points (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1) lie on line x+y=5 (slope -1). Since sunny lines cannot have slope -1, each of these points must be covered by a distinct sunny line. So we need at least 4 sunny lines, which we have exactly. So each line must cover one of those boundary points. Additionally, each line must also cover some interior points. Let's see if we can assign lines:\n\n- L1: passes through (1,4) and maybe (2,2) or (3,1) etc. Let's try to find a line through (1,4) and (2,2): slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. Equation: y = -2x + 6. At a=3, y = 0, not allowed. So L1 covers (1,4),(2,2). Good.\n\n- L2: passes through (2,3) and maybe (3,1): slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, allowed. Equation: y = -2x + 7. At a=1, y = 5, not allowed. So L2 covers (2,3),(3,1). Good.\n\n- L3: passes through (3,2) and maybe (4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-3) = -1, prohibited. So cannot pair (3,2) with (4,1). Could pair (3,2) with (2,1): slope = (1-2)/(2-3) = -1/-1 = 1, allowed. Equation: y - 2 = 1 (x - 3) => y = x - 1. At a=1, y = 0, not allowed. So L3 covers (3,2) and (2,1). Good.\n\n- L4: passes through (4,1) and maybe (1,1): slope = (1-1)/(4-1) = 0, prohibited. Could pair (4,1) with (2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/-2 = -0.5, allowed. Equation: y - 1 = -0.5 (x - 4) => y = -0.5 x + 3. At a=1, y = -0.5 + 3 = 2.5, not integer. So L4 covers (4,1) and maybe (3,? )? Let's see if L4 can cover (4,1) and (3,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-4) = 1/-1 = -1, prohibited. So L4 cannot cover any other required point besides (4,1). So L4 would only cover (4,1). That's okay, but we need to cover all interior points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Let's see which points are covered by L1, L2, L3, L4:\n\n- L1: (1,4),(2,2)\n- L2: (2,3),(3,1)\n- L3: (3,2),(2,1)\n- L4: (4,1) only\n\nNow covered points: (1,4),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(2,1),(4,1). Missing: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3). We have no lines left to cover those. So we need to adjust.\n\nWe could try to assign L4 to cover (1,1) as well. Let's see if we can find a line through (4,1) and (1,1) with slope = (1-1)/(4-1) = 0, prohibited. So cannot.\n\nWe could try to assign L1 to cover (1,1) instead of (1,4). But L1 must cover a boundary point. Let's try different pairings.\n\nWe need each line to cover one of the boundary points (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Let's denote them B1=(1,4), B2=(2,3), B3=(3,2), B4=(4,1). We need to assign each line to one of these B_i, and possibly also cover interior points.\n\nWe can try to pair each B_i with an interior point that lies on a sunny line with B_i.\n\nList interior points: I = {(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)}.\n\nWe need to find for each B_i a line L_i that passes through B_i and possibly some interior points.\n\nLet's examine each B_i:\n\n- B1 = (1,4). Possible interior points that can be paired with B1 on a sunny line (slope not 0, -1, infinite). For each interior point (a,b), compute slope = (b - 4)/(a - 1). Must not be 0, -1, infinite.\n\nList interior points and slopes:\n\n(1,1): slope undefined (vertical) because a=1 => same x coordinate, vertical line x=1, which is non-sunny (vertical). So cannot pair B1 with (1,1).\n\n(1,2): vertical line x=1, non-sunny.\n\n(1,3): vertical line x=1, non-sunny.\n\n(2,1): slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, allowed. So line through B1 and (2,1) slope -3.\n\n(2,2): slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So line through B1 and (2,2) slope -2.\n\n(3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed. So line through B1 and (3,1) slope -1.5.\n\nThus B1 can be paired with (2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Also could be paired with (1,? ) but those are vertical.\n\n- B2 = (2,3). Pair with interior points:\n\n(1,1): slope = (1-3)/(1-2) = -2/-1 = 2, allowed. So line through (2,3) and (1,1) slope 2.\n\n(1,2): slope = (2-3)/(1-2) = -1/-1 = 1, allowed. So line through (2,3) and (1,2) slope 1.\n\n(1,3): slope = (3-3)/(1-2) = 0/-1 = 0, prohibited (horizontal). So cannot pair with (1,3).\n\n(2,1): vertical line x=2, non-sunny.\n\n(2,2): vertical line x=2, non-sunny.\n\n(3,1): slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2/1 = -2, allowed. So line through (2,3) and (3,1) slope -2.\n\nThus B2 can be paired with (1,1),(1,2),(3,1).\n\n- B3 = (3,2). Pair with interior points:\n\n(1,1): slope = (1-2)/(1-3) = -1/-2 = 0.5, allowed. So line through (3,2) and (1,1) slope 0.5.\n\n(1,2): slope = (2-2)/(1-3) = 0/-2 = 0, prohibited.\n\n(1,3): slope = (3-2)/(1-3) = 1/-2 = -0.5, allowed. So line through (3,2) and (1,3) slope -0.5.\n\n(2,1): slope = (1-2)/(2-3) = -1/-1 = 1, allowed. So line through (3,2) and (2,1) slope 1.\n\n(2,2): vertical line x=2, non-sunny.\n\n(3,1): vertical line x=3, non-sunny.\n\nThus B3 can be paired with (1,1),(1,3),(2,1).\n\n- B4 = (4,1). Pair with interior points:\n\n(1,1): slope = (1-1)/(1-4) = 0/-3 = 0, prohibited.\n\n(1,2): slope = (2-1)/(1-4) = 1/-3 = -1/3, allowed. So line through (4,1) and (1,2) slope -1/3.\n\n(1,3): slope = (3-1)/(1-4) = 2/-3 = -2/3, allowed. So line through (4,1) and (1,3) slope -2/3.\n\n(2,1): slope = (1-1)/(2-4) = 0/-2 = 0, prohibited.\n\n(2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/-2 = -0.5, allowed. So line through (4,1) and (2,2) slope -0.5.\n\n(3,1): vertical line x=3, non-sunny.\n\nThus B4 can be paired with (1,2),(1,3),(2,2).\n\nNow we need to assign each B_i to a distinct line L_i, each line can be paired with at most one interior point (since a line can intersect each column at most once). However, a line could potentially intersect multiple interior points if they lie on the same line, but we need to check.\n\nWe need to cover all interior points as well. Let's try to find a matching.\n\nWe have interior points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). We need to cover them with the 4 lines L1..L4 (each line can cover at most one interior point per column). Since we have 4 lines, we can cover at most 4 interior points (one per line) if each line covers exactly one interior point. However, some lines could cover two interior points if they are in different columns (e.g., line through (2,1) and (3,2) covers two interior points). But we need to see if any such line also passes through a boundary point.\n\nLet's see if any line can pass through a boundary point and two interior points. For example, line through B1 (1,4) and interior points (2,1) and (3,2) would need to be collinear: slope from B1 to (2,1) is -3, from (2,1) to (3,2) is (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, not equal. So cannot.\n\nLine through B1 and (2,2) and (3,1): slope B1-(2,2) = -2, slope (2,2)-(3,1) = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not equal. So cannot.\n\nLine through B1 and (2,1) and (3,1): slope B1-(2,1) = -3, slope (2,1)-(3,1) = 0, not equal.\n\nThus B1 cannot be on a line that also passes through two interior points.\n\nSimilarly, B2 (2,3) could be on a line with interior points (1,1) and (3,1)? Let's check: slope B2-(1,1) = (1-3)/(1-2) = -2/-1 = 2. Slope (1,1)-(3,1) = (1-1)/(3-1) = 0, not equal. So no.\n\nB2 with (1,2) and (3,1): slope B2-(1,2) = (2-3)/(1-2) = -1/-1 = 1. Slope (1,2)-(3,1) = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, not equal.\n\nB2 with (1,1) and (3,2): slope B2-(1,1) = 2, slope (1,1)-(3,2) = (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2 = 0.5, not equal.\n\nThus B2 cannot be on a line with two interior points.\n\nB3 (3,2) with interior points (1,1) and (2,1): slope B3-(1,1) = (1-2)/(1-3) = -1/-2 = 0.5. Slope (1,1)-(2,1) = (1-1)/(2-1) = 0, prohibited. So no.\n\nB3 with (1,3) and (2,1): slope B3-(1,3) = (3-2)/(1-3) = 1/-2 = -0.5. Slope (1,3)-(2,1) = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, not equal.\n\nB3 with (1,1) and (2,2): slope B3-(1,1) = 0.5, slope (1,1)-(2,2) = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, not equal.\n\nThus B3 cannot be on a line with two interior points.\n\nB4 (4,1) with interior points (1,2) and (2,2): slope B4-(1,2) = (2-1)/(1-4) = 1/-3 = -1/3. Slope (1,2)-(2,2) = (2-2)/(2-1) = 0, prohibited. So no.\n\nB4 with (1,3) and (2,2): slope B4-(1,3) = (3-1)/(1-4) = 2/-3 = -2/3. Slope (1,3)-(2,2) = (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, prohibited. So no.\n\nThus each line can cover at most one interior point (in addition to its boundary point). So we have 4 lines, each can cover at most one interior point, so we can cover at most 4 interior points. But there are 6 interior points. So we cannot cover all interior points with 4 lines. Therefore k=4 is impossible for n=4. This argument generalizes: For any n, the boundary points are (i, n+1-i) for i=1..n. Each line can cover at most one boundary point (since any line containing two boundary points would have slope -1, prohibited). So each line can cover at most one boundary point. Since we have k sunny lines, we can cover at most k boundary points with sunny lines. The remaining n-k boundary points must be covered by non-sunny lines. The non-sunny lines can be diagonal (x+y=n+1) covering all boundary points, but that uses one non-sunny line. So we can cover all boundary points with one non-sunny line, leaving k sunny lines to cover interior points. However, interior points are those with a+b ≤ n. There are n(n-1)/2 interior points. The number of interior points is larger than k times something? Let's see.\n\nIf we use one diagonal line to cover all boundary points, we have k sunny lines left to cover interior points. The interior points form a triangle of size n-1. The same argument as for n=4 applies: each line can intersect each column at most once, and each line can cover at most one interior point per column. The interior triangle has columns a=1..n-1, with column a having n-a points. The number of interior points in column a is n-a. For a=1, there are n-1 interior points (points (1,b) for b=1..n-1). Since each line can intersect column 1 at most once, we need at least n-1 lines to cover all interior points in column 1. But we have only k sunny lines. So we need k ≥ n-1. But k is at most n-1 (since we used one diagonal line). So we need k = n-1 to cover interior points. That would mean k = n-1, which for n≥5 is ≥4. So for n=5, k=4 is less than n-1=4? Actually n-1=4, so k=4 equals n-1. So maybe k=4 works for n=5? Let's examine.\n\nFor n=5, interior triangle has columns a=1..4, with column sizes: a=1: 4 points, a=2: 3 points, a=3: 2 points, a=4: 1 point. So column 1 has 4 interior points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). We have k=4 sunny lines (since we used one diagonal line for boundary). Each sunny line can intersect column 1 at most once, so we can cover at most 4 points in column 1, which matches the number of interior points in column 1. So it's possible in principle. For n=5, k=4 might be possible if we can assign each sunny line to a distinct point in column 1 and also cover the other interior points in columns 2..4. This is similar to the problem we attempted earlier for n=5 interior triangle with 4 sunny lines. We attempted to find a covering and struggled, but maybe it's possible.\n\nThus the general impossibility argument for k≥4 might be more subtle. The solution's claim \"k≥4 impossible by column counting\" might be based on the fact that each line can intersect each column at most once, and there are n columns, each with at least one point, so we need at least n lines to cover all points if we restrict to sunny lines? But we have n lines total, but we have non-sunny lines that can cover many points per column. So the column counting argument might be that if we have k sunny lines, then we can cover at most k points in each column (since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once). The remaining points in each column must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, covering entire columns. So we need at least (n+1-a - k) vertical lines for column a? Something like that. Let's try to formalize.\n\nLet’s denote columns a=1..n. Column a has n+1-a points. Suppose we have k sunny lines. In column a, each sunny line can intersect at most one point, so at most k points can be covered by sunny lines. The remaining n+1-a - k points must be covered by non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical (cover entire column) or horizontal/diagonal (cover at most one point per column). So to cover the remaining points, we need at least (n+1-a - k) non-vertical non-sunny lines (horizontal/diagonal) or vertical lines. However, vertical lines can cover all points in a column, but each vertical line can only be used for one column. So we need at least one vertical line for each column where we need to cover more than one point beyond the sunny lines. Since the interior columns have many points, we need many vertical lines.\n\nIf we have n-k non-sunny lines total, we can allocate at most n-k vertical lines, covering at most n-k columns fully. For the remaining columns, we need to cover the remaining points using horizontal/diagonal lines, each covering at most one point per column. So the total number of points we can cover in those columns is limited.\n\nLet's try to derive a bound: Suppose we have k sunny lines and n-k non-sunny lines. Let V be the number of vertical lines among the non-sunny lines (0 ≤ V ≤ n-k). Then we have H = n-k-V non-vertical non-sunny lines (horizontal or diagonal). Each vertical line can cover all points in a column. Each horizontal/diagonal line can intersect each column at most once. So the total number of points covered by vertical lines is sum_{i in columns covered by vertical lines} (n+1-i). The total number of points covered by horizontal/diagonal lines is at most H * n (since each such line can intersect each column at most once). The total number of points covered by sunny lines is at most k * n (since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once). So total coverage capacity is at most sum_{i in V columns} (n+1-i) + (H + k) * n.\n\nWe need this to be at least total number of points N = n(n+1)/2.\n\nWe can try to maximize coverage capacity given constraints. The worst case for covering is when we allocate vertical lines to the largest columns (small i). So V columns are i=1..V. Then sum_{i=1}^{V} (n+1-i) = sum_{j=n+1-V}^{n} j = (sum_{j=1}^{n} j) - (sum_{j=1}^{n-V} j) = n(n+1)/2 - (n-V)(n-V+1)/2.\n\nThus total capacity = [n(n+1)/2 - (n-V)(n-V+1)/2] + (H + k) * n.\n\nSince H = n-k-V, we have H + k = n - V. So total capacity = [n(n+1)/2 - (n-V)(n-V+1)/2] + (n - V) * n.\n\nSimplify: total capacity = n(n+1)/2 - (n-V)(n-V+1)/2 + n(n - V).\n\nCompute n(n - V) = n^2 - nV.\n\nSo total capacity = n(n+1)/2 + n^2 - nV - (n-V)(n-V+1)/2.\n\nCombine n(n+1)/2 + n^2 = (n^2 + n)/2 + n^2 = (3n^2 + n)/2.\n\nThus total capacity = (3n^2 + n)/2 - nV - (n-V)(n-V+1)/2.\n\nNow compute (n-V)(n-V+1)/2 = ((n-V)^2 + (n-V))/2 = ((n-V)^2)/2 + (n-V)/2.\n\nThus total capacity = (3n^2 + n)/2 - nV - ((n-V)^2)/2 - (n-V)/2.\n\nCombine terms: Write everything over 2:\n\ntotal capacity = [3n^2 + n - 2 nV - (n-V)^2 - (n-V)] / 2.\n\nExpand (n-V)^2 = n^2 - 2 nV + V^2.\n\nSo total capacity = [3n^2 + n - 2 nV - (n^2 - 2 nV + V^2) - (n - V)] / 2 = [3n^2 + n - 2 nV - n^2 + 2 nV - V^2 - n + V] / 2 = [ (3n^2 - n^2) + (n - n) + (-2 nV + 2 nV) - V^2 + V ] / 2 = [2 n^2 - V^2 + V] / 2 = n^2 - (V^2 - V)/2.\n\nThus total capacity = n^2 - (V^2 - V)/2.\n\nWe need total capacity ≥ total points N = n(n+1)/2 = (n^2 + n)/2.\n\nThus we need n^2 - (V^2 - V)/2 ≥ (n^2 + n)/2 => Multiply both sides by 2: 2 n^2 - (V^2 - V) ≥ n^2 + n => n^2 - (V^2 - V) ≥ n => n^2 - n ≥ V^2 - V => (n^2 - n) ≥ V(V - 1).\n\nSince V ≤ n - k (since V ≤ n - k), we can bound V. But we need to see if there is any V that satisfies this inequality for given n and k.\n\nWe want to see if for given k, there exists a feasible V (0 ≤ V ≤ n - k) such that the inequality holds. If no such V exists, then k is impossible.\n\nSimplify inequality: V(V - 1) ≤ n(n - 1). Since n(n - 1) = n^2 - n. So V(V - 1) ≤ n(n - 1). This holds for any V ≤ n, because V(V - 1) is increasing in V for V ≥ 1, and V ≤ n implies V(V - 1) ≤ n(n - 1). So the inequality always holds. So the capacity bound does not restrict k. So this counting argument is not sufficient.\n\nThus the solution's claim \"k≥4 impossible by column counting\" might be based on a more refined argument that we haven't captured. But we need to verify if the claim is indeed correct. Let's try to find a construction for n=5, k=4 (i.e., 4 sunny lines, 1 non-sunny line). We attempted earlier and struggled, but maybe there is a construction.\n\nWe can try to use the diagonal line x+y=6 (non-sunny) to cover boundary points (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). Then we need to cover interior points (a,b) with a+b ≤5 using 4 sunny lines. This is the same as the interior triangle for n=5 we attempted earlier. We tried to find a covering and found it difficult. Let's try a systematic search for n=5 interior triangle with 4 sunny lines.\n\nWe have interior points S = {(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1)}. We need to cover S with 4 sunny lines.\n\nWe can think of this as a hitting set problem: each line is defined by a pair of points (or a single point). Since we have only 4 lines, each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So we need to cover 10 points with 4 lines, each covering at most 4 points, so we need to achieve average coverage of 2.5 points per line. So some lines must cover 3 points, others 2.\n\nWe can try to find lines that cover 3 points. For a line to cover 3 points, it must intersect three columns, each at a point. So we need three points (a1,b1),(a2,b2),(a3,b3) that are collinear and have distinct a-values. Let's search for triples of points in S that are collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nWe can enumerate all triples of points in S and check if they are collinear with slope not 0, -1, infinite.\n\nPoints in S:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3)\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2)\nColumn 4: (4,1)\n\nWe need triples with distinct a-values.\n\nLet's list possible triples:\n\n- (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) but (3,3) not in S (since 3+3=6 >5). So not.\n\n- (1,1),(2,3),(3,5) not in S.\n\n- (1,2),(2,1),(3,0) not in S.\n\n- (1,2),(2,3),(3,4) not in S.\n\n- (1,3),(2,1),(3,-1) not.\n\n- (1,3),(2,2),(3,1): these are (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). Are they collinear? Compute slope between (1,3) and (2,2): (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, prohibited. So not sunny.\n\n- (1,3),(2,3),(3,3) not.\n\n- (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not.\n\n- (1,4),(2,3),(3,2): points (1,4),(2,3),(3,2). Slope between (1,4) and (2,3) = (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, prohibited. So not sunny.\n\n- (1,4),(2,2),(3,0) not.\n\n- (1,1),(2,1),(3,1): vertical line x=1? Actually points (1,1),(2,1),(3,1) are horizontal line y=1, prohibited.\n\n- (1,2),(2,2),(3,2): horizontal line y=2, prohibited.\n\n- (1,3),(2,3),(3,3): horizontal line y=3, prohibited.\n\n- (1,4),(2,4) not in S.\n\n- (1,1),(2,2),(4,4) not.\n\n- (1,2),(2,3),(4,5) not.\n\n- (1,1),(3,2),(5,3) not.\n\n- (1,2),(3,2),(5,2) not.\n\n- (1,1),(3,1),(5,1) not.\n\n- (1,4),(3,2),(5,0) not.\n\n- (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) not.\n\n- (2,2),(3,2),(4,2) not.\n\n- (2,3),(3,2),(4,1): points (2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Are they collinear? Compute slope between (2,3) and (3,2): (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, prohibited. So not sunny.\n\n- (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) not.\n\n- (2,2),(3,1),(4,0) not.\n\n- (2,1),(4,1) vertical line x=2? Actually (2,1) and (4,1) are horizontal line y=1, prohibited.\n\n- (2,2),(4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, allowed. That's a line through (2,2) and (4,1). Does it also pass through a point in column 3? Let's compute line equation: slope m = -0.5, passes through (2,2): y - 2 = -0.5 (x - 2) => y = -0.5 x + 3. At x=3, y = -1.5 + 3 = 1.5, not integer. So line covers only (2,2) and (4,1). That's 2 points.\n\n- (2,3),(4,1): slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, prohibited.\n\n- (3,1),(4,1) vertical line x=3? Actually (3,1) and (4,1) are horizontal line y=1, prohibited.\n\n- (1,1),(3,2),(5,3) not.\n\n- (1,2),(3,2),(5,2) not.\n\n- (1,3),(3,2),(5,1): points (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). Compute slope between (1,3) and (3,2): (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed. Check if (5,1) lies on same line: line equation: y - 3 = -0.5 (x - 1) => y = -0.5 x + 3.5. At x=5, y = -2.5 + 3.5 = 1, yes! So points (1,3),(3,2),(5,1) are collinear with slope -0.5. However, (5,1) is not in S (since a=5 >4). So not relevant.\n\n- (1,4),(3,2),(5,0) not.\n\n- (1,1),(4,1) vertical line x=1? Actually (1,1) and (4,1) are horizontal line y=1, prohibited.\n\n- (1,2),(4,1): slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, allowed. That's line L: y = -1/3 x + 7/3. At x=2, y = -2/3 + 7/3 = 5/3, not integer. At x=3, y = -1 + 7/3 = 4/3, not integer. So only covers (1,2) and (4,1).\n\n- (1,3),(4,1): slope = (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3, allowed. At x=2, y = -2/3*2 + something? Let's compute line: y - 3 = -2/3 (x - 1) => y = -2/3 x + 2/3 + 3 = -2/3 x + 11/3. At x=2, y = -4/3 + 11/3 = 7/3, not integer. At x=3, y = -2 + 11/3 = 5/3, not integer. So only (1,3),(4,1).\n\n- (1,4),(4,1): slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, prohibited.\n\nThus there are no triples of points in S that are collinear with a sunny slope. So any line can cover at most 2 points from S. Actually we found some lines covering 2 points: e.g., line through (1,3) and (3,2) covers (1,3) and (3,2). Also line through (1,2) and (4,1) covers (1,2) and (4,1). Also line through (2,2) and (4,1) covers (2,2) and (4,1). Also line through (1,1) and (3,2) slope 0.5 covers (1,1) and (3,2). Also line through (1,1) and (2,2) slope 1 covers (1,1) and (2,2). Also line through (1,2) and (2,3) slope 1 covers (1,2) and (2,3). Also line through (1,3) and (2,2) slope -1, prohibited. So many lines cover 2 points.\n\nThus we need to cover 10 points with 4 lines, each covering at most 2 points, which gives a maximum of 8 points covered. So it's impossible! Wait, we need to verify that no line can cover 3 points. We found no triple collinear with sunny slope. So each line can cover at most 2 points. Since we have 4 lines, we can cover at most 8 points, but we need to cover 10 points. Therefore it's impossible to cover the interior triangle with 4 sunny lines. This proves that k=4 is impossible for n=5.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k≥4 impossible is correct, at least for n=5. For larger n, the interior triangle will have more points, and the same argument may apply: each line can intersect each column at most once, and there are many points in column 1 (n-1 points). Since each line can intersect column 1 at most once, we need at least n-1 lines to cover all interior points in column 1. If we have only k sunny lines (and we used one diagonal line for boundary), we need k ≥ n-1. For n≥5, k=4 n-1 (since n-1≥4 for n≥5). For n=5, n-1=4, so k=4 equals n-1, which is borderline. But we just proved that even with k=n-1=4, it's impossible because each line can cover at most 2 points, not enough to cover all interior points. For larger n, the interior triangle has even more points, and the bound becomes stricter: each line can cover at most 2 points? Actually for larger n, there might be lines covering more than 2 points, but we need to check.\n\nLet's consider n=6 interior triangle (a+b ≤6). Points: column 1: (1,1)-(1,5) (5 points), column 2: (2,1)-(2,4) (4 points), column 3: (3,1)-(3,3) (3 points), column 4: (4,1)-(4,2) (2 points), column 5: (5,1) (1 point). Total interior points = 5+4+3+2+1 = 15. We have k=5 sunny lines (if we use one diagonal line for boundary). Each line can intersect each column at most once, so each line can cover at most 5 points (one per column). So 5 lines can cover at most 25 points, which is enough. But we need to check if we can cover all interior points with 5 lines. However, we also need to cover column 1 points (5 points) with 5 lines, each line covering one column 1 point. So each line must be assigned a distinct column 1 point. Then each line can also cover points in other columns. So it's plausible that k=n-1 works for n≥5? But we need to check if there is a construction for n=6, k=5. The solution says k≥4 impossible for all n≥3, which would include n=6, k=5. But we need to verify if k=5 is indeed impossible for n=6. Let's try to see if we can find a construction for n=6, k=5.\n\nWe can try to generalize the pattern we used for n=5 interior triangle: we found that each line can cover at most 2 points because there were no triples collinear. For larger n, there might be triples collinear. For example, points (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) are collinear with slope 1, but (3,3) is not interior for n=5 (since 3+3=6 >5). For n=6, interior includes points with a+b ≤6, so (3,3) is allowed (since 3+3=6). So line y = x passes through (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). That's three interior points collinear with slope 1, which is sunny. So a line can cover 3 points. Similarly, line y = x + 1 passes through (1,2),(2,3),(3,4) but (3,4) not interior (3+4=7 >6). So only line y = x covers three interior points. Also line y = x - 1 passes through (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) but (4,3) not interior (4+3=7 >6). So only line y = x covers three interior points. So we can have a line covering 3 points.\n\nThus for n=6, we might be able to cover interior points with 5 lines.\n\nBut the solution claims k≥4 impossible for all n≥3. That seems too strong. Let's test n=6, k=5. If we can find a construction, then the answer {0,1,2,3} is wrong. Let's try to construct for n=6.\n\nWe have points (a,b) with a+b ≤7 (since n+1 = 7). Boundary points are (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1). Use diagonal line x+y=7 (non-sunny) to cover all boundary points. Then interior points are those with a+b ≤6. That's the triangle of size n-1=5. Points:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) (5 points)\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4) (4)\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) (3)\nColumn 4: (4,1),(4,2) (2)\nColumn 5: (5,1) (1)\n\nTotal interior points = 5+4+3+2+1 = 15.\n\nWe have k=5 sunny lines to cover these 15 points. Each line can intersect each column at most once, so each line can cover at most 5 points (one per column). So 5 lines can cover at most 25 points, enough. But we need to ensure we can cover all points.\n\nWe need to assign each line to a distinct column 1 point (since there are 5 column 1 points). So each line will cover one column 1 point. Then each line can also cover points in columns 2..5.\n\nWe need to find 5 lines L_i passing through (1,b_i) for b_i = 1..5, each with a slope m_i (not 0, -1, infinite) such that the union of points (a, b_i + (a-1) m_i) for a=2..5 (if integer and within allowed range) covers all interior points.\n\nWe can try to find such lines.\n\nOne approach: Use lines of slope 1: y = x + c. For column 1, points (1,b) => b = 1 + c => c = b - 1. So lines of slope 1 passing through (1,b) have equation y = x + (b - 1). For each b, this line will intersect column a at point (a, a + (b - 1)). This point is allowed if a + (b - 1) ≤ n+1 - a = 7 - a. So condition: a + b - 1 ≤ 7 - a => 2a + b ≤ 8 => b ≤ 8 - 2a. For each a, we need b ≤ 8 - 2a. For a=2, b ≤ 8 - 4 = 4. So for column 2, line with b ≤ 4 will intersect interior. For a=3, b ≤ 8 - 6 = 2. So for column 3, b ≤ 2. For a=4, b ≤ 8 - 8 = 0, so no interior points for a≥4. So lines of slope 1 through (1,b) with b ≤ 4 will intersect column 2; with b ≤ 2 will intersect column 3; none intersect column 4 or 5. So slope 1 lines can cover at most columns 1,2,3.\n\nSimilarly, lines of slope -1/2: y = -0.5 x + c. For (1,b): b = -0.5 + c => c = b + 0.5. Then point at column a: y = -0.5 a + b + 0.5 = b + 0.5 - 0.5 a. For integer a, we need y integer => b + 0.5 - 0.5 a integer => (b - 0.5 a) + 0.5 integer => b - 0.5 a must be half-integer. So b and a must have opposite parity. For a=2, b must be integer? Let's compute: y = b + 0.5 - a/2 = b + 0.5 - 1 = b - 0.5. So y integer => b must be half-integer? Actually b is integer, so b - 0.5 is half-integer, not integer. So slope -0.5 lines cannot intersect column 2 at integer points. So not useful.\n\nWe need to find lines that intersect many columns.\n\nPerhaps we can use lines of slope 1 to cover column 1,2,3 points. Then use other slopes to cover column 4,5 points.\n\nLet's try to assign lines:\n\n- L1: slope 1 through (1,1) => equation y = x. This covers (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). (2,2) and (3,3) are interior points. Good.\n\n- L2: slope 1 through (1,2) => y = x + 1. This covers (1,2),(2,3). (3,4) not interior. So L2 covers (1,2),(2,3).\n\n- L3: slope 1 through (1,3) => y = x + 2. This covers (1,3),(2,4). (3,5) not interior. So L3 covers (1,3),(2,4).\n\n- L4: slope 1 through (1,4) => y = x + 3. This covers (1,4),(2,5) not interior (2+5=7 >6). So L4 only covers (1,4). Not good.\n\n- L5: slope 1 through (1,5) => y = x + 4. This covers (1,5),(2,6) not interior. So L5 only covers (1,5).\n\nThus using slope 1 lines, we cover many interior points: (2,2),(3,3),(2,3),(2,4). We still need to 